# data/users.json — INTENTIONALLY TRACKED despite being listed here.
# (v10.501 Phase 2 Arc C Batch 4c — closes GAP-002 via Path B
# accept-and-document.)
#
# The file is currently tracked in git history. It contains bcrypt
# password hashes (post-v10.497) and envelope-wrapped hashes
# (post-v10.500 Batch 3c), NOT plaintext. The previous comment here
# called the contents "plaintext credentials — replaced by hashed
# users in v10.497"; that wording was misleading on two counts
# (the file is now bcrypt-backed, AND it remained tracked despite
# the apparent gitignore entry).
#
# Why this entry exists at all:
#   - If the file is ever removed from the working tree (e.g. on a
#     fresh clone where the operator bootstraps from scratch), this
#     entry prevents it from being silently re-added by `git add .`
#     before the operator decides whether to bootstrap from a
#     generator or restore from a secure source.
#
# Why it's still tracked:
#   - Bootstrap-from-generator workflow (Path A) requires a
#     `generate_staff_v2.py` first-run prerequisite documented in
#     README + a separate sync mechanism for the operator's existing
#     user records. That work hasn't been designed.
#   - Path B (this batch) accepts current behaviour, documents the
#     inconsistency, and defers Path A to a future arc gated on
#     repo privacy posture changes.
#
# Operational discipline (codified in OPERATIONAL_PROTOCOL.md
# "Intentionally-tracked credential data"): do NOT run
# `git rm --cached data/users.json` to "fix" the apparent
# inconsistency. The tracking is intentional. If you genuinely want
# to switch to Path A, that becomes its own arc with a designed
# bootstrap-from-generator workflow.
data/users.json
